---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import QrLessonOverlay from '../../components/QrLessonOverlay.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="You scanned a QR code | NoRec.US"
  description="NoRec.US documents surveillance in Illinois. This landing page also explains what an untrusted QR code can reveal or lead you to before taking you to the underlying research."
>
  <QrLessonOverlay />

  <main class="qr_shell">
    <section class="qr_intro">
      <p class="eyebrow">YOU FOUND NOREC.US</p>
      <h1>The information is real. The QR code is part of the lesson.</h1>
      <p class="qr_intro_lede">
        NoRec.US documents surveillance systems, policies, contracts, public records,
        and the unanswered questions around them. If you scanned one of our QR codes,
        you can read the material immediately — no gate, login, or surprise download.
      </p>
      <p>
        But a QR code is still a trust decision. The pattern itself is usually just
        encoded data; the risk is what the phone, browser, or app is asked to do with
        that data next.
      </p>

      <figure class="qr_intro_rick">
        <img
          src="/media/qr/rickroll-roll.gif"
          alt="Animated Rick Astley Never Gonna Give You Up clip used as a Rickroll joke."
          loading="eager"
        />
        <figcaption>This site chose a harmless lesson instead.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="qr_warning">
      <p class="eyebrow">WHAT COULD A RANDOM CODE DO?</p>
      <h2>Treat QR destinations like links you cannot read until you decode them.</h2>

      <div class="qr_warning_grid">
        <div>
          <strong>Tracking links can log the scan.</strong>
          <p>
            A unique redirect or URL can tell an operator which poster, card, or location
            was scanned and can receive ordinary connection metadata such as a timestamp,
            IP address, and browser/device information.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong>Send you to phishing.</strong>
          <p>
            A code can open a convincing login, payment, delivery, parking, or account page
            whose domain is not the organization you thought you were dealing with.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong>Replace a payment destination.</strong>
          <p>
            A sticker placed over a legitimate code can redirect a payment or account setup
            to somebody else without changing the sign, meter, menu, or machine underneath it.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong>Prompt an app, permission, or download.</strong>
          <p>
            A destination can try to open a deep link, convince you to install software,
            download a file, add a profile, or grant a permission you did not intend to give.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong>Reach vulnerable software.</strong>
          <p>
            Security researchers have demonstrated exploit chains delivered through crafted
            links and QR-driven workflows. That does not mean decoding an ordinary QR code
            automatically compromises a modern phone; it means untrusted destinations deserve
            the same caution as other untrusted links, and updates matter.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <strong>Simply annoy the hell out of you.</strong>
          <p>
            Sometimes the payload is not malware or credential theft. Sometimes somebody
            merely gives you a song you are going to remember every time you see a QR code.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="qr_media" aria-label="A brief visual lesson in QR-code trust">
      <figure>
        <img
          src="/media/qr/random-qr-superbowl-meme.png"
          alt="Meme joking about people scanning a random QR code and discovering it is a cyberattack."
          loading="eager"
        />
        <figcaption>Unknown code, unknown destination.</figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="qr_warning">
      <p class="eyebrow">WHAT HAPPENED HERE</p>
      <h2>This could have been far less friendly.</h2>
      <p>
        A malicious operator could have used a unique redirect to correlate a scan with a
        particular sticker or location, sent you to a credential phish, prompted a download,
        or attempted to exploit vulnerable software. NoRec did none of that. This page serves
        static information, and the small lesson card only plays a deliberately obnoxious
        audio file hosted on NoRec.US if you explicitly click the demonstration button.
      </p>
      <p class="qr_reassurance">
        <strong>The practical rule:</strong> preview the destination when your scanner allows it,
        check the domain, keep your device updated, and be especially suspicious when an
        unexpected scan asks for credentials, payment, software, a profile, or unusual permissions.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="qr_turn">
      <p class="eyebrow">NOW FOR WHY THE CODE WAS HERE</p>
      <h2>Illinois credential photos and facial-recognition access.</h2>
      <p>
        If you have an Illinois driver’s license, permit, or state ID, the Secretary of State
        maintains a photograph captured through that credential process. Illinois law also
        expressly addresses facial-recognition search services involving those photographs.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>The credential population is not adult-only.</strong> Illinois says there is no
        minimum age for a state ID card, and instruction permits may be issued beginning at age
        15 under specified conditions.
      </p>
      <div class="hero_actions">
        <a class="button button_dark button_big" href="/surveillance/face/illinois-id-photos/">See the Illinois evidence →</a>
        <a class="button button_light" href="/illinois/">Browse Illinois surveillance</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="qr_receipt_panel">
      <p class="eyebrow">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
      <div class="receipt_row"><span>DOCUMENTED</span><strong>Illinois maintains captured credential photographs.</strong></div>
      <div class="receipt_row"><span>DOCUMENTED</span><strong>State law expressly addresses facial-recognition search services.</strong></div>
      <div class="receipt_row"><span>DOCUMENTED</span><strong>The credential population includes minors.</strong></div>
      <div class="receipt_row receipt_unknown"><span>UNKNOWN</span><strong>Exact historical retention, template architecture, and total search volume.</strong></div>
    </section>

    <section class="qr_rule">
      <p class="eyebrow">THE RULE</p>
      <h2>If the evidence does not establish it, NoRec does not present it as fact.</h2>
      <p>
        NoRec.US documents government surveillance systems using statutes, policies, contracts,
        public records, government reports, and other source material. Unknowns stay unknown
        until the record answers them.
      </p>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
